  "text": "The midwife had seen many births, but none quite like this. As the infant drew his first breath, a shimmering, parchment-like skin peeled away from his back, revealing not flesh, but a detailed cartography of the kingdom. The lines were not ink, but veins of silver light that pulsed in rhythm with his tiny heart. The court cartographers, summoned in haste, fell to their knees in awe. They recognized the coastlines, the mountain ranges, and the hidden valleys that had been lost to history for centuries. It was not merely a map; it was a living document, shifting and updating in real-time. When the king’s armies marched, the silver lines on the boy’s back would brighten, tracing their paths. When a storm threatened the northern ports, the map would darken, swirling with ominous clouds. The king, initially terrified by the omen, soon realized the potential. His son was not just a prince; he was the kingdom’s compass. Yet, the map held a secret that unsettled the royal advisors. In the center of the territory, where the capital stood, there was a blank space, a void that refused to be filled. As the boy grew, the blankness expanded, swallowing the surrounding details. Whispers spread through the court that the map was not showing where the kingdom was, but where it was going. The king’s third son, with his back turned to the world, carried the weight of a destiny that was still being written, a future that no one could predict, only watch as it unfolded in silver and shadow."
