  "text": "The silence in the throne room was heavier than the stone walls that enclosed it. King Aldric stared at his son, not with the pride of a father, but with the cold calculation of a ruler. The boy, barely a year old, lay in the crystal cradle, his back exposed to the flickering torchlight. The silver veins pulsed, a rhythmic heartbeat that seemed to echo in the minds of every courtier present. It was beautiful, undeniably so, but it was also terrifying. The map was not static; it was a prophecy made flesh. The blank space in the center, where the capital should have been, had grown larger overnight. It was no longer a void; it was a hunger. The High Priest, an old man with eyes like clouded glass, stepped forward, his staff tapping against the marble floor. \"Your Majesty,\" he whispered, his voice trembling, \"the map does not show what is. It shows what will be. The capital is not disappearing; it is being erased from the future. The kingdom is not losing its heart; it is losing its soul.\" Aldric felt a chill crawl up his spine. He had always believed in the permanence of his dynasty, in the unshakeable foundation of his rule. But looking at the expanding darkness on his son’s back, he realized that permanence was an illusion. The silver lines were not just tracing paths; they were carving out a destiny that no amount of gold or steel could stop. The boy opened his eyes, and for a fleeting second, they were not the brown of his mother or the blue of his father, but a swirling, starry void. The map on his back flared bright, and the blank space consumed the last of the surrounding details. The kingdom was not ending; it was changing, and the king was merely a spectator to the unfolding of a new, unknown world."
}

