  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.",
  "text": "The silence that followed was not an absence of sound, but a heavy, physical weight that pressed against the eardrums of the remaining courtiers. They stood frozen, their ceremonial armor clinking softly as they shifted, a trivial noise that seemed obscenely loud in the vacuum of the throne room. Aldric did not move. He could not. His mind was a fractured mirror, reflecting a thousand possible futures, all of them ending in the same absolute void. The infant, now silent, lay in the High Priest’s arms, its eyes closed, the silver veins on its back receding into the pale skin like retreating tides. The torches flickered, their flames turning a sickly, bruised purple, casting long, distorted shadows that danced with a malicious intent. The High Priest lowered his gaze, his expression unreadable, a mask of stone carved by centuries of secrets. He spoke again, his voice a dry rasp that scraped against the air. \"It is done, Your Majesty. The cycle is complete. The old world ends, and the new one begins.\" Aldric tried to speak, to command, to beg, but his throat was a locked gate. He looked at his hands, large and calloused from years of ruling, and saw them trembling, not with fear, but with the realization of his own insignificance. He was a relic, a dust mote in the eye of a storm that had no name. The map on the boy’s back was gone, leaving only a faint, scar-like outline, a reminder of the power that had just been unleashed. The king closed his eyes, accepting the darkness that was already creeping into the corners of his vision, knowing that when he opened them, the world he had known would be unrecognizable, a ghost story told by a stranger in a land that no longer existed."
}

